>
  <Header />

  <section class="bg-gopro-charcoal text-white py-16 border-b-4 border-gopro-orange">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <nav class="text-sm mb-6 font-sans">
        <a href="/" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Home</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <a href="/guides" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Guides</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <span class="text-white">Stucco & Humidity</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-4 tracking-tight">Stucco & Humidity in Central Florida</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl font-sans">
        How Central Florida's humidity affects stucco homes. Mildew, cracking, paint failure, and what to do about it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-padding">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6 font-sans">
            Most <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a>, <a href="/areas/orlando" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Orlando</a>, and Central Florida homes built in the last 30 years have stucco exteriors. Stucco handles Florida heat well, but the constant humidity creates problems: green mildew growth, hairline cracks that let water behind the finish, and paint that peels early if you don't prep properly.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Why Stucco in Florida?</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Stucco (cement-based exterior finish) is cheap, fast to install, and handles heat without warping like vinyl siding. That's why most tract homes in <a href="/areas/celebration" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Celebration</a>, <a href="/areas/poinciana" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Poinciana</a>, and <a href="/areas/st-cloud" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">St. Cloud</a> use it. The problem isn't stucco itself—it's how Florida's humidity interacts with it over time.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">The Humidity Problem</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Mildew & Algae Growth</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Central Florida averages 70-80% humidity year-round. North-facing stucco walls that never get direct sun stay damp longer after rain, and mildew loves damp surfaces. You'll see green or black streaks, especially near the roof line, around AC units, or in shaded areas.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Mildew isn't just cosmetic. It eats into paint and eventually into the stucco itself if you ignore it. <a href="/services/power-washing" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Power washing</a> removes it, but it grows back within a year unless you treat the surface with mildewcide or repaint with mildew-resistant paint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Paint Adhesion Issues</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Stucco is porous. It absorbs moisture from humidity and rain. If you <a href="/services/interior-exterior-painting" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">paint</a> over damp stucco or skip primer, the paint bonds poorly. Within 6-12 months, you'll see bubbling, peeling, or flaking—especially on walls that get afternoon sun, which bakes the paint while moisture tries to escape from underneath.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gopro-orange/10 border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
              <strong>Key rule:</strong> Always power wash stucco before painting, wait 48-72 hours for it to dry completely, then prime bare spots before applying finish coats. Rushing this process guarantees early paint failure in Florida's climate.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Hairline Cracks</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Stucco cracks. It's normal—houses settle, temperature swings cause expansion and contraction, and the cement-based finish is rigid. Hairline cracks (under 1/8 inch) are cosmetic if they're just in the surface layer. Wider cracks can let water penetrate behind the stucco, which causes bigger problems: wood rot, mold inside walls, and eventually structural damage.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Most Kissimmee and Orlando tract homes have minor cracking after 10-15 years. We fill cracks with elastomeric caulk (flexible, moves with the stucco), texture-match the repair, and paint over it. If cracks are widespread or opening wider than 1/4 inch, you may have foundation movement or water damage behind the stucco—call a structural inspector before repainting.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">How to Maintain Stucco in Central Florida</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">1. Power Wash Every 12-18 Months</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Regular <a href="/services/power-washing" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">power washing</a> removes mildew, dirt, and algae before they stain permanently. Use medium pressure—too high and you'll damage the stucco surface. If your house has heavy mildew, a soft wash with mildewcide cleaner works better than pure pressure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">2. Repaint Every 5-7 Years</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Quality exterior paint on stucco lasts 5-7 years in Central Florida if applied correctly. Use 100% acrylic paint with built-in mildewcide and UV blockers. Elastomeric paint (flexible, thicker) costs more but bridges hairline cracks and lasts longer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">3. Fix Cracks Early</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Small cracks become big cracks. Water gets in, freeze-thaw cycles (yes, even in Florida—occasional cold snaps), and expansion/contraction widen them. Fill cracks when you notice them, don't wait until the next paint cycle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">4. Keep Gutters Clean</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Clogged gutters overflow during Florida's summer storms, pouring water down stucco walls. That keeps walls damp, feeds mildew growth, and eventually causes water intrusion. Clean gutters = drier walls = less mildew.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">When to Worry</h2>
          
          <div class="space-y-4 my-8 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="text-2xl text-gopro-orange">⚠️</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Cracks wider than 1/4 inch</strong> or cracks that are growing. This suggests foundation movement or structural issues beyond cosmetic repair.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="text-2xl text-gopro-orange">⚠️</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Soft or hollow-sounding stucco</strong> when you tap it. This indicates the stucco has separated from the underlying substrate or water damage behind it.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="text-2xl text-gopro-orange">⚠️</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Water stains inside the house</strong> on walls that have stucco on the outside. Water is getting through, and you need to find the entry point before it causes mold or rot.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="text-2xl text-gopro-orange">⚠️</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Paint peeling in sheets</strong> less than 2 years after painting. This usually means the stucco wasn't dry when painted, or cheap paint was used.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Vacation Rental Owners</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            If you own a <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a> or <a href="/areas/celebration" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Celebration</a> vacation rental near Disney, curb appeal matters for bookings. Green mildew-streaked stucco looks neglected in listing photos. Budget for annual power washing and repaint every 5-7 years. Clean, fresh-painted stucco photographs well and signals to guests that the property is maintained.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Stucco is a great exterior finish for Florida heat, but Central Florida's humidity creates maintenance needs: regular power washing to control mildew, proper paint prep (dry time, primer, quality paint), and filling cracks before they widen. Ignore these basics, and you're repainting every 2-3 years instead of 5-7.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-8 my-8">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide">Need Stucco Power Washed or Repainted?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-6">
              We power wash, repair cracks, prime, and paint stucco with Florida-rated coatings. Serving Kissimmee, Orlando, and Central Florida since 2014.
            </p>
            <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
              <a href="/estimate" class="btn-primary">
                Get Free Estimate
              </a>
              <a href="tel:4072446873" class="btn-secondary">
                Call (407) 244-6873
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-gray-200">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-sans">
              <a href="/guides" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">← Back to All Guides</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTASection
    title="Stucco Maintenance & Repair"
    description="Power washing, crack repair, and quality paint. Serving Central Florida."
  />

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
